<!--
  Every package version this repository resolves, decided in one file.

  Imported automatically by every project in this tree, the way
  Directory.Build.props is, and it exists for the same class of reason: a
  version written in more than one project file is a version that can disagree
  with itself, and the disagreement is never visible in the file you are
  reading. Eleven project files each naming Microsoft.Data.SqlClient is eleven
  chances to bump ten of them.

  This is not a hypothetical. CI's -warnaserror gate records having caught
  "NuGet downgrade conflicts across the three projects", and
  ConnectorState.Dashboard carries a long comment explaining why it pins two
  Azure packages it never calls - it had settled on Azure.Identity's own floor
  of 1.11.4 while the rest of the solution carried 1.21.0. Both are the same
  failure: one package at two versions because two project files each made
  their own decision, and neither file could see the other.

  WHAT THIS FILE CHANGES ABOUT WRITING A PROJECT FILE.

  A PackageReference here carries Include and its metadata and no Version. A
  Version attribute on a PackageReference is now NU1008, an error, so the split
  this file removes cannot be reintroduced by hand without the build saying so.
  CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled is false below, which closes the other
  door: VersionOverride would let a single project opt out silently, which is
  precisely the arrangement being removed.

  WHY TRANSITIVE PINNING IS ON.

  Most of the version skew in this solution was never written down anywhere.
  Nothing referenced Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens; it arrived under
  Microsoft.Data.SqlClient in one project and under Microsoft.Graph in another,
  at different versions, and the only place the split was visible at all was the
  offline package set - see the identity block at the bottom of this file.
  CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled makes every PackageVersion below a
  floor for transitive arrivals too, so a package named here resolves to one
  version across the whole solution or the restore fails.

  It is a floor and never a ceiling: if some dependency requires a HIGHER
  version than the one named here, NuGet reports NU1605 rather than quietly
  downgrading it. That failure means "raise the entry", not "remove the pin".

  WHAT IT COSTS TO GET WRONG. build/Get-OfflinePackages.ps1 is a transcript of
  the resolved graph, checked against it in CI by build/Test-OfflinePackageList.ps1.
  Every id resolving at two versions is two entries in that list, two nupkgs on
  the air-gapped build machine, and two advisory trails to follow the next time
  one of them is named in a CVE. Adding a package here is therefore a decision
  about that list as much as about a build, and the list is regenerated in the
  same change:

      dotnet restore SqlTicketsConnector.sln
      pwsh build/Test-OfflinePackageList.ps1 -Configuration Base -Update

  on Windows - a different RID resolves a different graph.

  ONE MORE PLACE THIS FILE HAS TO REACH. The release package carries a buildable
  copy of the tree under source\, and Build.ps1 stages it by copying every file
  that is not under bin\, obj\ or the other excluded folders - so this file
  travels with it, and a package built today restores and builds from source\
  exactly as a clone does. Removing it from that copy does not degrade
  gracefully: restore stops with NU1015 naming every project and every package
  reference in it. Build.ps1 asserts that Directory.Build.props reached the
  staged tree, for the same reason; this file now belongs on that list too.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
    <CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>false</CentralPackageVersionOverrideEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The connector contract, and the gRPC stack that carries it. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Google.Protobuf tracks the code generator, not the newest release.
      Grpc.Tools 2.40.0 emits code against the 3.18.0 runtime, and that pairing
      is what the checked-in Contracts/ stubs are built from.

      Serilog.Sinks.OpenTelemetry requires 3.26.1 or later, so with the exporter
      enabled the two constraints cannot both hold at 3.18.0 and restore fails
      with NU1605. Enabling the exporter therefore raises the protobuf runtime
      to the current 3.x. That is safe in this direction: the C# runtime is
      backward compatible with older generated code, and the generator itself is
      unchanged, so the contract types are byte-identical either way.

      The decision is made here rather than in SqlTicketsConnector.csproj
      because SqlTicketsConnector is not the only project with Google.Protobuf
      in its graph - the test project reaches it through a ProjectReference -
      and with transitive pinning on, a version chosen in one project file and
      not the other is a downgrade error rather than a disagreement. Both halves
      move together because -p:EnableOtlpExporter=true is a global property and
      is therefore visible here.

      CI builds the solution in both configurations so neither can rot.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.18.0" Condition="'$(EnableOtlpExporter)' != 'true'" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.35.1" Condition="'$(EnableOtlpExporter)' == 'true'" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Core" Version="2.40.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Grpc.Tools" Version="2.40.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Identity, secrets and the Graph SDK. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Core" Version="1.54.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Identity" Version="1.21.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets" Version="4.11.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Graph" Version="5.105.0" />

    <!--
      An advisory floor rather than a feature: GHSA-7j59-v9qr-6fq9 /
      CVE-2026-44503 (High) in the Kiota RedirectHandler, fixed in 1.22.0, while
      Microsoft.Graph 5.105.0 still asks for 1.21.1. Remove it once the Graph SDK
      catches up.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Kiota.Abstractions" Version="1.22.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Data access. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      One version, everywhere, and it is not a preference. Two SqlClients in one
      output directory is a load conflict rather than a merge, and two in one
      estate is two advisory trails to follow. Five project files named this
      package before this file existed.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="5.2.2" />

    <!--
      Cloudera's Hive and Impala drivers are ODBC drivers and there is no
      maintained .NET client for HiveServer2's Kerberos SASL transport, so this
      is the supported path rather than a convenience. The driver itself is an
      operator-installed MSI under Cloudera's own licence and is never bundled.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Data.Odbc" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Logging. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog" Version="4.4.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.Console" Version="6.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.EventLog" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="7.0.0" />

    <!-- Only referenced under -p:EnableOtlpExporter=true; see the note above. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Serilog.Sinks.OpenTelemetry" Version="4.1.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Traces and metrics, as against the log records the Serilog sink above
    carries. Referenced only by PushCore, and only under
    -p:EnableOtlpExporter=true.

    Declared UNCONDITIONALLY, at one version, unlike Google.Protobuf a few
    entries up. That entry needs two branches because two things disagree about
    it; these two are referenced by nothing when the flag is off, so a version
    named here has nothing to conflict with and a condition would only be
    another thing that can be wrong.

    1.18.0 rather than the 1.13.x this was first written against, and the reason
    is not novelty: 1.13.1 restores with three NU1902 moderate-severity advisory
    warnings against the exporter package itself, and CI builds with
    -warnaserror. The current release clears all three.

    Both move together. The SDK and its exporter are versioned in lockstep by
    the project that publishes them, and a mismatched pair fails at the
    AddOtlpExporter extension method rather than at restore.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry" Version="1.18.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="OpenTelemetry.Exporter.OpenTelemetryProtocol" Version="1.18.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The seven Microsoft.Extensions.* packages the OpenTelemetry SDK drags in.
    Nothing in this repository calls them; they are pinned because they were
    FLOATING, and floating in the one direction this file exists to stop.

    OpenTelemetry asks for 9.0.0. On net10.0 something else in the graph lifted
    them to 10.0.0; on net9.0 nothing did, so 9.0.0 stood. Same solution, same
    commit, two target frameworks, two answers - which is two sets of nupkgs on
    an air-gapped machine and two advisory trails the next time one of them is
    named in a CVE. Exactly the failure the header of this file describes,
    arriving through a transitive edge nobody had reason to look at.

    It was invisible until the offline package list learned to check both target
    frameworks; the net10.0 check alone had been passing on it for as long as
    the exporter has existed.

    10.0.3 rather than 10.0.0, so the whole Microsoft.Extensions family carries
    one version: the .Abstractions, .Options and .Primitives packages beside
    them already resolve at 10.0.3 through the Graph SDK. A version named here
    is a floor, so this raises net9.0 to meet net10.0 rather than the reverse -
    the safe direction, and the one that leaves a single set to download.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.EnvironmentVariables" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Configuration" Version="10.0.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" Version="10.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Only referenced under -p:EnablePdfExtraction=true. PDF is the one common
    format with no answer in the base class library, and adding a parser is a
    dependency decision rather than a coding one: PdfPig is Apache-2.0, which
    this repository can redistribute, and the copyleft and per-seat commercial
    extractors cannot be. Regenerate the offline list in the same change that
    turns it on.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Test. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.9.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ===========================================================================
    The identity libraries, which nothing in this repository references.

    They are here because they were the largest split in the solution, and it
    was a split no project file admitted to. Read from the restore graph before
    this block existed:

        Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 5.2.2  ->  Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens 6.35.0
                                            Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 6.35.0
        Microsoft.Graph.Core 3.2.5      ->  Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect 8.15.0
        Microsoft.Identity.Client 4.83.1 -> Microsoft.IdentityModel.Abstractions 8.14.0

    Every project carries SqlClient or Azure.Identity, so every project had the
    6.x line in its graph. The projects that also carry the Graph SDK -
    PushCore and everything above it - had it lifted to 8.15.0 by Graph.Core;
    the three that do not - Connector.Security, ConnectorState.Dashboard and
    SqlTicketsConnector, the agent-hosted connector that must never gain a Graph
    dependency - settled on SqlClient's floor and stayed at 6.35.0.

    The result was seven ids resolved at two versions each, carried twice in the
    offline package set: Abstractions (8.14.0 and 8.15.0), and JsonWebTokens,
    Logging, Protocols, Protocols.OpenIdConnect, Tokens and
    System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt (6.35.0 and 8.15.0). Microsoft.IdentityModel.Validators
    8.6.1 rides along on the Graph half as an eighth member of the family, at
    one version, and is deliberately left where Graph.Core puts it.

    RAISING RATHER THAN LOWERING, and the reason it is not a gamble: the
    combination being asked for here already ships. PushCore.Sql, PushCore.State,
    SqlGraphPush and SqlHierarchyPush each reference Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
    5.2.2 directly and each already resolved this family at 8.15.0, because
    Graph.Core lifted them. SqlClient's 6.35.0 is a floor it states, not a
    ceiling it enforces, and the halves of this solution that run against 8.15.0
    are the halves that talk to a database.

    Pinning here rather than adding PackageReference lines to three project
    files is the point of the file: nothing in those projects calls this code,
    and a direct reference to a package a project does not use is a claim that
    goes stale the moment the reason for it does.
    ===========================================================================
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Abstractions" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.JsonWebTokens" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Logging" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Protocols.OpenIdConnect" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens" Version="8.15.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt" Version="8.15.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
